Q1. What is Git, and how is it different from SVN?

Answer:
Git is an open-source distributed version control system that tracks and manages source code changes.
Unlike SVN (centralized VCS), Git allows every developer to have a local repository with full history.
This enables offline work, faster operations, and less dependency on the remote server.

Example:

SVN needs a connection to the central server to commit.
Git commits locally first and can push later when online.


Q2. What is the difference between git pull and git fetch?

Answer:

git fetch downloads the latest changes from the remote repo but does not merge them into your local branch.

git pull performs both: fetch + merge, updating your working branch automatically.

Use Case:
Use fetch when you want to review changes first, and pull when you’re ready to update your branch.


Q3. How can you change the last commit message in Git?
git commit --amend -m "New corrected commit message"


Q4. How do you undo the last commit but keep your changes in the working directory?
git reset --soft HEAD~1



How do you create a tag in Git, and what is the difference between a lightweight tag and an annotated tag?

Level 1 — Git Fundamentals (30 Questions + Answers)
Q1. What is Git?

Git is a distributed version-control system that tracks code changes and lets multiple developers collaborate.
Each developer has a full local copy of the repository, making work possible even without a network connection.

Example:

git init


Creates a new Git repository in the current directory.

Q2. Difference between Git and GitHub?

Git → command-line tool for version control.

GitHub → cloud service that hosts Git repositories and adds features like pull requests and issue tracking.

Q3. Difference between clone and fork?

Clone: make a local copy of a repository.

Fork: duplicate another user’s repo under your GitHub account to propose changes independently.

Q4. What does git add do?

Moves modified files from the working directory to the staging area.

git add file1.js
git add .

Q5. git commit vs git push

git commit → records changes locally.

git push → sends committed changes to a remote branch.

Q6. Purpose of .gitignore

Lists files/folders Git should skip—build artifacts, logs, credentials, etc.
Example pattern:

node_modules/
*.log
.env

Q7. How to check repository status?
git status


Shows staged, unstaged, and untracked files.

Q8. git diff usage

Displays line-by-line changes between files, commits, or branches.

git diff
git diff main..feature

Q9. git init vs git clone

init: start a brand-new repo.

clone: copy an existing remote repo.

Q10. What is HEAD in Git?

HEAD is a pointer to your current commit (usually the latest commit of your checked-out branch).

Q11. How to create a branch?
