Level 2 — Intermediate Git (35 Questions + Answers)
Q31. What is a branch in Git?

A branch is an independent line of development that lets you isolate work from the main codebase.

Commands:

git branch feature/login
git checkout feature/login

Q32. How does branching actually work?

A branch is just a pointer to a commit (a reference). When you commit, the branch pointer moves forward to the new commit.

Q33. How do you merge one branch into another?

Switch to the target branch and run:

git checkout main
git merge feature/login


If there are no conflicting changes, Git performs a fast-forward merge.

Q34. What is a fast-forward merge?

If the target branch has not diverged, Git simply moves its pointer forward—no merge commit is created.

Q35. What is a three-way merge?

When both branches diverge, Git creates a new merge commit that combines the two histories using the common ancestor.

Q36. What are merge conflicts?

Conflicts occur when the same line(s) are modified in both branches. Git marks them with <<<<<<<, =======, and >>>>>>>.
Resolve, stage, and then complete the merge:

git add .
git merge --continue

Q37. How do you abort a merge?
git merge --abort


Restores the branch to the state before the merge started.

Q38. What is rebasing?

Rebasing re-applies commits on top of another base branch, creating a linear history.

git checkout feature
git rebase main

Q39. When should you use rebase vs merge?

Rebase before pushing, to keep local history clean.

Merge for shared branches, to preserve full context.

Q40. What is an interactive rebase?

Lets you modify commit history (reword, squash, drop):

git rebase -i HEAD~5

Q41. How do you squash commits?

During an interactive rebase, change subsequent pick to squash and save. The commits combine into one.

Q42. How do you abort a rebase?
git rebase --abort

Q43. What is git stash used for?

Temporarily saves uncommitted work so you can switch branches safely.

git stash
git stash pop

Q44. How do you list or apply specific stashes?
git stash list
git stash apply stash@{2}

Q45. How do you delete stashes?
git stash drop stash@{0}
git stash clear

Q46. What does git revert do?

Creates a new commit that reverses a specific earlier commit—safe for shared branches.

git revert <commit_sha>

Q47. How does git reset differ from git revert?

Reset: moves HEAD backward, can erase commits (unsafe).

Revert: adds a new commit that undoes previous changes (safe).

Q48. What is the difference between git reset --soft, --mixed, and --hard?
Mode	Effect
soft	Keep staged + working changes
mixed	Unstage but keep in working dir
hard	Discard all changes completely
Q49. What is git reflog and how do you use it?

Shows every movement of HEAD; used to recover lost commits.

git reflog
git branch recover <sha>

Q50. How do you restore a lost commit using reflog?

Find the commit hash in reflog and create a branch:

git branch restore <sha>

Q51. What is git cherry-pick?

Applies a specific commit from another branch onto the current branch.

git cherry-pick <sha>

Q52. How do you cherry-pick multiple commits?
